Equivalence Groups Found
└── Modbus TCP client for reading/writing PLC registers
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, pyModbusTCP, modbus-tk, minimalmodbus
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient(host, port=port), client.connect(), client.read_holding_registers(address, count, unit=unit), 
    │   client.write_register(address, value, unit=unit), response.isError(), response.registers[0]
    ├── Pitfalls   : pymodbus returns a response object with .isError() and .registers; pyModbusTCP's read_holding_registers returns a plain list or None on 
    │   failure (no isError method); minimalmodbus's read_register/write_register API uses instrument objects tied to serial ports, not TCP host/port 
    │   constructors, requiring a full rewrite of connection setup; modbus-tk uses a Master/query-based API (execute() with function codes) rather than named 
    │   methods like read_holding_registers; unit/slave id parameter naming and position differs across libraries (unit= vs slave= vs address as positional); 
    │   error handling model differs: exceptions vs None-returns vs isError() flags, requiring adapter logic to preserve current IOError-raising behavior
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All four packages provide Modbus master/client functionality capable of reading holding registers and writing single registers over a network or serial
        link, which is the core operation performed in ModbusIO. pymodbus's TCP client is the primary target; pyModbusTCP and modbus-tk offer similar 
        TCP-focused client APIs and could substitute with a compatibility shim. minimalmodbus is explicitly called out in the file's own docstring as a 
        substitution target, though it is serial (RTU)-oriented and exposes a very different Instrument-based API.

